.TH sh "" "Command"
.SH NAME
sh \- command language interpreter
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBsh [\-eiknstuvx] [\fIfile\fB [\fIarg ...\fB]]\fR
.br
\fBsh \-c \fIstring \fB[\fIarg ...\fB]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
sh -- command language interpreter
Usage:	sh [option ...] token ...
Options:
	-c cmds	Read commands from 'cmds'
	-e	Exit on any error if noninteractive
	-i	Interactive even if no tty attached
	-k	Place all keyword args into global environment
	-n	Read commands but do not execute them
	-s	Read commands from stdin, write output to stderr
	-t	Read and execute one command only
	-u	Report error if actual value of shell variable is null
	-v	Print each line as read
	-x	Print each command and argument as executed
	-	Cancel -v and -x options
The following reserved tokens may not be used in the first
position of the command unless quoted:
	case do done elif else fi for in then until while { } ( )
If the first token is not reserved, it is treated as the name of a command.
The remaining tokens are treated as arguments.
The characters * ? [ ] specify patterns that match file names.
To quote characters or strings, these escape characters are provided:
	'...' "..." \\
Each 'token', unless quoted, is checked for substitutions.
.HE
.B sh
reads commands, either from
.I file
(with any following
.I arg
words made available as its positional parameters), from the
argument of
.BR \-c ,
or, if neither is given, from its standard input, and executes them.
If the first character of argument 0 is
.BR \- ,
.B sh
is a
.IR "login shell" :
before reading anything else it executes the commands in
.B /etc/profile
and then, if the variable
.B HOME
names a directory containing a file
.BR .profile ,
the commands in
.BR $HOME/.profile .
.PP
A
.I command
is a sequence of
.I tokens
(words) separated by blanks or tabs.
The first token names the command to execute; the remaining tokens are
passed to it as arguments, after the substitutions described below.
Commands may be combined with the following operators:
.IP "\fIcommand\fB | \fIcommand\fR" 1i
Pipe: connect the standard output of the first command to the
standard input of the second.
.IP "\fIcommand\fB ; \fIcommand\fR"
Run the first command to completion, then the second.
.IP "\fIcommand\fB &\fR"
Run
.I command
in the background, without waiting for it; print its process id.
A background command's standard input is
.B /dev/null
unless redirected.
.IP "\fIcommand\fB && \fIcommand\fR"
Run the second command only if the first returns a zero (successful)
exit status.
.IP "\fIcommand\fB || \fIcommand\fR"
Run the second command only if the first returns a nonzero exit status.
.PP
Any of these commands may be redirected:
.IP "\fB> \fIfile\fR" 1i
Write the standard output to
.IR file ,
creating it if need be and discarding any previous contents.
.IP "\fB>> \fIfile\fR"
Append the standard output to
.IR file .
.IP "\fB< \fIfile\fR"
Take the standard input from
.IR file .
.IP "\fB<< \fIword\fR"
Read a
.IR "here document" :
take the standard input from the following lines of the shell input,
up to but not including a line that consists solely of
.IR word .
Unless
.I word
is quoted, parameter and command substitution are performed on the
document.
.IP "\fIn\fB> \fIfile\fR, \fIn\fB< \fIfile\fR, \fIn\fB>> \fIfile\fR"
Like the above, but redirect file descriptor
.I n
instead of 1 (output) or 0 (input).
.IP "\fIn\fB<& \fIm\fR, \fIn\fB>& \fIm\fR"
Duplicate file descriptor
.I n
from
.IR m .
.IP "\fIn\fB<&\-\fR, \fIn\fB>&\-\fR"
Close file descriptor
.IR n .
.PP
A token that contains any of the characters
.BR ? ,
.BR * ,
or
.B [
is a file-name pattern:
.B ?
matches any one character,
.B *
matches any string of characters (including none), and
\fB[\fIchars\fB]\fR
matches any one of the enclosed characters (a range may be written
.IR c1 \- c2 ).
.B sh
replaces such a token with the sorted list of file names in the
current directory that match it; if none match, the token is passed
through unchanged.
A leading
.B .
in a file name, and every
.BR / ,
must be matched explicitly.
.PP
The characters
.BR \e ,
.BR ' ,
and
.B "\ "
quote (turn off the special meaning of) other characters.
.B \e
quotes the single character that follows it; a
.B \e
immediately followed by a newline is discarded, letting a command be
continued onto the next line.
A pair of single quotes (\fB'\fR ... \fB'\fR) quotes everything between
them.
A pair of double quotes (\fB"\fR ... \fB"\fR) also quotes everything
between them, except that
.B $
substitutions (below) still occur inside them.
.PP
A token of the form
\fB$\fIname\fR,
\fB${\fIname\fB}\fR,
or
\fB$\fIn\fR
(a single digit
.IR n ,
1 through 9) is replaced by the value of the shell variable
.I name
or of positional parameter
.IR n .
The forms
\fB${\fIname\fB\-\fIword\fB}\fR,
\fB${\fIname\fB=\fIword\fB}\fR,
\fB${\fIname\fB+\fIword\fB}\fR,
and
\fB${\fIname\fB?\fIword\fB}\fR
substitute
.I word
if
.I name
is unset (and, for
.BR = ,
also assign it
.IR word );
.B +
substitutes
.I word
only if
.I name
.I is
set; and
.B ?
substitutes
.I word
(or a default message) and aborts the shell if
.I name
is unset.
The following special parameters are also recognized:
.RS
.IP \fB$0\fR 0.5i
The name the shell was invoked with.
.IP \fB$#\fR
The number of positional parameters.
.IP \fB$*\fR
All positional parameters.
.IP \fB$@\fR
All positional parameters; inside double quotes, each becomes a
separate word.
.IP \fB$\-\fR
The options currently in effect.
.IP \fB$?\fR
The exit status of the last command.
.IP \fB$!\fR
The process id of the last background command.
.IP \fB$$\fR
The process id of the shell itself.
.RE
.PP
A command enclosed in grave accents (\fB\`\fR ... \fB\`\fR) is run, and
the token containing it is replaced by its standard output (trailing
newlines removed).
.PP
.B sh
recognizes the following reserved words as the start of a compound
command, if unquoted and in the position of a command name:
.RS
.IP "\fBfor \fIname \fB[in \fIword ...\fB] do \fIcommand-list \fBdone\fR" 0.5i
Set
.I name
to each
.I word
in turn (or to each positional parameter, if
.B in
is omitted) and run
.I command-list
once per value.
.IP "\fBcase \fIword \fBin \fIpattern\fB[|\fIpattern\fB]...) \fIcommand-list\fB;; ... esac\fR"
Run the
.I command-list
of the first
.I pattern
that matches
.IR word .
.IP "\fBwhile \fIcommand-list \fBdo \fIcommand-list \fBdone\fR"
.IP "\fBuntil \fIcommand-list \fBdo \fIcommand-list \fBdone\fR"
Run the second
.I command-list
as long as (or until) the first returns a zero exit status.
.IP "\fBif \fIcommand-list \fBthen \fIcommand-list \fBfi\fR"
The usual conditional; zero or more
.B elif
.I command-list
.B then
.I command-list
clauses and one final
.B else
.I command-list
may appear before the closing
.BR fi .
.IP "\fB( \fIcommand-list \fB)\fR"
Run
.I command-list
in a subshell.
.IP "\fB{ \fIcommand-list \fB}\fR"
Run
.I command-list
in the current shell (without forking a subshell).
.RE
.PP
.B break
\fB[\fIn\fB]\fR
and
.B continue
\fB[\fIn\fB]\fR
leave, or skip to the next iteration of, the
.IR n th
enclosing
.BR for ,
.BR while ,
or
.B until
loop (default the innermost).
.SH "SPECIAL COMMANDS"
The following commands are built into
.B sh
and are executed without a
.BR fork :
.IP "\fB: \fI[token ...]\fR" 1i
Do nothing (successfully); the arguments are evaluated but ignored.
.IP "\fB. \fIfile\fR"
Read and execute commands from
.I file
(searched for along
.BR PATH ),
in the current shell, without positional parameters of its own.
.IP "\fBcd \fI[dir]\fR"
Change the working directory to
.I dir
(default
.BR HOME ).
.IP "\fBeval \fI[token ...]\fR"
Run the tokens, joined together, as a command.
.IP "\fBexec \fI[command [arg ...]]\fR"
Apply any redirections and then, instead of forking, replace the
shell with
.I command
(or, with no
.IR command ,
apply the redirections to the current shell).
.IP "\fBexit \fI[n]\fR"
Terminate the shell with exit status
.I n
(default the status of the last command).
.IP "\fBexport \fI[name ...]\fR"
Mark each
.I name
so that its value is passed to the environment of commands the shell
runs; with no arguments, print the exported names and values.
.IP "\fBreadonly \fI[name ...]\fR"
Mark each
.I name
so that it cannot be reassigned; with no arguments, print the
read-only names and values.
.IP \fBlogin\fR
.IP "\fBnewgrp \fI[arg ...]\fR"
Replace the shell with
.B /bin/login
or
.BR /bin/newgrp .
.IP "\fBread \fIname ...\fR"
Read one line from the standard input and assign successive
blank-separated fields to the
.I name
list, the last
.I name
receiving whatever is left over.
.IP "\fBset [\-eiknstuvx] [\fIarg ...\fB]\fR"
With no arguments, print the value of every shell variable.
Otherwise, set the option flags exactly as on the command line, and
reset the positional parameters to
.IR arg ...
.IP "\fBshift \fI[n]\fR"
Discard the first
.I n
(default 1) positional parameters, renumbering the rest from 1.
.IP \fBtimes\fR
Print the accumulated user and system time of the shell's children.
.IP "\fBtrap \fI[command] [n ...]\fR"
Arrange for
.I command
to be run when the shell catches signal
.IR n .
With
.I command
omitted, restore the default action for each
.IR n .
Signal 0 means the shell's own exit.
With no arguments at all, print the traps now set.
.IP "\fBumask \fI[nnn]\fR"
Set the file-creation mask to the octal value
.IR nnn ;
with no argument, print the current mask.
.IP "\fBwait \fI[pid]\fR"
Wait for the background process
.I pid
(default: all background processes) to finish.
.SH "COMMAND-LINE OPTIONS"
.IP "\fB\-c \fIcmds\fR"
Read commands from the following argument,
.IR cmds ,
instead of from a file or the standard input.
.IP \fB\-e\fR
If the shell is not interactive, exit as soon as any command returns
a nonzero status or is not found.
.IP \fB\-i\fR
Treat the shell as interactive (print prompts, and ignore
.B SIGTERM
and
.BR SIGINT )
even if its input is not a terminal.
.IP \fB\-k\fR
Put all keyword (variable=value) arguments of a command into that
command's environment, not just the ones that precede the command
name.
.IP \fB\-n\fR
Read and parse commands but do not execute them; useful for checking
a script's syntax.
.IP \fB\-s\fR
Read commands from the standard input even if file or
.B \-c
arguments are also given, and write ordinary output to the standard
error.
.IP \fB\-t\fR
Read and execute a single command, then stop.
.IP \fB\-u\fR
Treat the reference to an unset shell variable as an error, instead
of substituting the null string.
.IP \fB\-v\fR
Echo each input line as it is read.
.IP \fB\-x\fR
Echo each command and its arguments, after substitution, as it is
executed.
.IP \fB\-\fR
Turn off the
.B \-v
and
.B \-x
options.
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 20n
\fB/etc/profile\fR	commands run by every login shell
\fB$HOME/.profile\fR	commands run by a user's login shell
\fB/dev/null\fR	standard input of a background command
\fB/tmp/sh*\fR	temporary files (here documents, and so on)
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B test
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B sh
writes a diagnostic to the standard error for a syntax error, an
unknown command, an illegal redirection, or an illegal variable name;
a syntax error terminates a noninteractive shell.
.B sh
returns the exit status of the last command it ran, or the status
given to
.BR exit .
.SH NOTES
Positional parameters above
.B $9
cannot be referenced directly; use
.B shift
to bring later parameters into range 1-9.
.PP
The built-in default for
.B PATH
is
.BR :/bin:/usr/bin ,
which searches the current directory first; a script or interactive
user that depends on this should set
.B PATH
explicitly.
.PP
There is no shell-function facility, no arithmetic expansion, and no
\fB$(\fIcommand\fB)\fR
form of command substitution in this
.BR sh ;
use grave accents for command substitution.
